cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use occulta in your work, please cite it, together with the data sets and catalogues that it lists for each result."
title: "occulta: a blind search of TESS, K2 and Kepler photometry for uncatalogued transiting planets and eclipsing binaries"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Aggarwal
    given-names: Keshav
    email: keshavaggarwal2310@gmail.com
version: 0.0.1
license: MIT
# Concept DOI on Zenodo: all versions, always resolves to the newest release
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23634486
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23634486
    description: "Concept DOI (all versions; resolves to the newest release)"
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23634487
    description: "Version DOI of release v0.0.1"
date-released: 2026-10-25
abstract: >-
  occulta is a Python package for a blind search of the photometry of TESS, K2 and Kepler
  for transiting planets and eclipsing binaries that no published catalogue contains. It
  searches every light curve of the stars in a chosen brightness range for individual dips
  and periodic signals, stitches dips from different sectors into candidates, vets each
  candidate as a planet, and analyses stellar signals as eclipsing binaries: ephemerides,
  eclipses fitted one by one, secondary phases and eccentricities, eclipse timing compared
  between pipelines, assignment of the eclipses to their star with difference images of
  the TESS pixels, uniform photometry of the full-frame images, and comparison of eclipse
  timing with Gaia DR3 astrometric orbits. The user chooses planet hunting, binary
  hunting, or both, and any detection can be repeated from public data with one command.
repository-code: "https://github.com/jovian-explorer/occulta"
url: "https://jovian-explorer.github.io/software.html#occulta"
keywords:
  - exoplanets
  - eclipsing binaries
  - TESS
  - Kepler
  - K2
  - Gaia
  - photometry
  - transit search
